WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:05.170
I grew up in the american south..

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:10.070
Maybe not the heart of it, but close enough
to see and experience a setting and environment

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:15.750
around me, that I caught closer glimpses of
when my family traveled deeper into the south…

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:30.339
a setting and environment that is very rarely
represented in movies.

00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:36.430
This is why I think I’ve been so drawn to
writer and director Jeff Nichols’ filmography.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:41.949
A native of arkansas, there’s a character
that ties all of Nichols’ films together.

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:45.550
And I’m not talking about Michael Shannon,
although their five film collaboration has

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.940
produced some of his best roles.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:54.059
All of Nichols films are set and shot in the
south, with the exception of Take Shelter

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:56.460
(but more on that one later).

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.750
Shotgun Stories, Nichols first film sets up
the story of a feud between broken families

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:02.750
in arkansas.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:08.620
The decay of the area helps mirror the lack
of hope for anything better that drives the

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:16.540
characters towards conflict.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:23.250
Nichols third film Mud, is as much named after
the setting as the main character.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:28.250
Set and shot along the Mississippi river in
Arkansas the location seeps through every

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:34.560
pore of the film.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.350
Midnight Special spans more territory than
Nichols’ other films, but placing the climactic

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:48.990
moments in Louisiana, brings the director
back into comfortable territory.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:54.090
And finally with Loving, set in Virginia near
where I grew up, Nichols looks at the south

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.990
not just as a setting but as a subject.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.953
Bringing some of the underlying conflicts
of the location out of subtext, and addressing

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:00.953
them clearly.

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:04.190
When I watched Mud, the first of Nichols films
that I saw, I immediately recognized a part

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.470
of the south that I’d seen, and experienced
in real life, but had never seen reflected

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:06.470
in a film before.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.020
The south isn’t a favorite of Hollywood’s
but it’s gotten it’s fair share of exposure

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.110
over the years.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.579
However, it’s representation is limited.

00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:18.480
Most films have portrayed the historic south,
an idealized idyllic version of the south,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.030
or an upper class version of the south.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.409
But none of these were the south I knew.

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:28.170
Jeff Nichols image of the south is a south
that I recognize.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:34.590
It’s a south where disenchanted men work
manual labor jobs, where the idea of “living

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:40.480
off the land” still exists as an ideal even
after industrialization reared its head, and

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.400
then left the area in shambles.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.791
It’s a south where the touch of man did
not create beautiful cities and parks, but

00:02:47.791 --> 00:02:53.129
came, used the land and it’s inhabitants,
and left scars and broken people.

00:02:53.129 --> 00:02:58.060
Where men, now abandoned by other richer men,
do what they can to scrape by.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.770
It’s a troubled place with it’s problems.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.959
But it’s people, perhaps inexplicably, love
it.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:06.770
They find identity and pride in the place.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:15.450
They dream, not of escaping, but of bettering
themselves and the land where they grew up.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.810
This south is one where a return to nature
is still revered.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:25.409
Nichols shows all this, the beauty and the
scars, with the same loving gaze.

00:03:25.409 --> 00:03:28.249
There isn’t a judgement, just a tenderness.

00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:29.829
For the people, for the places.

00:03:29.829 --> 00:03:33.659
He doesn’t shy away from the damage and
the problems, but he also doesn’t inflate

00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:38.431
them into caricatures or suck all humanity
out of the setting.

00:03:38.431 --> 00:03:41.689
I’ve seen some use the term “Southern
Gothic” to describe Nichols films.

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:43.859
And that strikes me as a little funny.

00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:48.120
The elements present in Nichols’ south,
if they were exaggerated could constitute

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.439
southern gothic.

00:03:49.439 --> 00:03:55.620
But anyone who describes Nichols work as such
hasn’t seen how real his south is.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.710
These themes are so grounded in Nichols work
that even when he sets a story outside of

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:05.430
the south, like he did with Take Shelter,
set and shot in Ohio, he still looks at the

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.330
land and the people with the same gaze.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:13.889
I even found Take Shelter incorrectly included
on a list of “best southern movies.”

00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:21.340
Ohio becomes a facsimile of Nichols image
of the south, differentiated only by name.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.700
Nichols uses the visual language of his films
to communicate aspects of the settings.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:32.260
In shotgun stories and in Loving, the stillness
and slowness of the pacing communicate the

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.950
slower pace of life.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:37.220
Often shots don’t move, they just rest.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:43.340
And he’s films are mostly shot outside under
natural sunlight, leaning heavily on nature

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.630
for locations.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:52.190
In Mud he uses slow steadicam movement throughout
the film to mimic the slow flowing of the

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:56.640
mississippi river, reinforcing how it’s
at the heart of this area.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.180
Nichol’s south isn’t a complete picture
of the place, it isn’t meant to be.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.560
But it’s a picture of a facet of an area
I rarely see anywhere else.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:09.810
It’s a picture that obviously Nichols himself
is in love with.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.820
One that clearly is meaningful to him.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:17.340
And I think it’s one that clearly is put
forth by someone who understands the place,

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.180
who themselves, is a product of the south.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:23.410
I’m not a die hard fan of the south, perhaps
because I wasn’t born there.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.390
You wouldn’t have to do much to lure me
back into the north or out west.

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:31.200
But the image of those who are rooted in this
place is one that I recognize and one that

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.320
I can sympathize with.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.510
And I think it’s one that’s rarely understood
well enough to be portrayed accurately.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:41.580
I don’t think hollywood has intentionally
ignored this image of the south.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.370
It’s just a matter of writing what you know.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:52.340
And it took someone who understood that image
to film there and express it clearly.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.990
I think this shows the importance of localized
voices.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:06:02.020
How smaller artists can represent an area
authentically in a way nobody else has.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:19.510
And I hope Nichols is the kind of director
we’ll see more 

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:53.060
of 

00:06:53.060 --> 00:07:16.380
in 
the future of film.

